course: geography
lesson: 45
title: Tsunamis
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A tsunami is not one giant surfing wave: it is a series of huge waves made when something suddenly shoves a vast amount of sea water, usually the sea floor jerking up or down in an undersea earthquake.
explain: In the deep ocean a tsunami races along as fast as a jet plane, yet it may be less than a metre high, so a ship can ride over it without noticing.
explain: Near the coast the water gets shallow, the wave slows down and piles up, sometimes over 10 metres high, and floods far inland like the whole sea climbing onto the land.
explain: Slosh the water at one end of a full bath with your hand: the wave crosses low and fast, then heaps up and spills over at the shallow end; a tsunami does that with a whole ocean.
explain: Often the sea first drains far back from the beach, which tempts people to go and look; the right thing is to run to high ground at once, because the waves come in a train and the first is often not the biggest.
fact: A tsunami is a series of huge waves caused by something suddenly moving a lot of water, usually an undersea earthquake.
fact: In the deep ocean a tsunami travels as fast as a jet plane, about 700-800 km/h, yet is often less than a metre high.
fact: Near the shore a tsunami slows down and piles up, sometimes over 10 metres high, and floods far inland.
fact: Often the sea suddenly drains far back from the beach just before a tsunami arrives.
fact: A tsunami is a train of several waves, minutes apart, and the first is often not the biggest.
fact: The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami, after a magnitude 9.1 earthquake off Sumatra, killed about 230,000 people in 14 countries.
fact: In 2011 a magnitude 9 earthquake sent a tsunami into Japan that killed about 18,000 people and damaged the Fukushima nuclear plant.
fact: Tsunami warning centres use earthquake sensors and pressure gauges on the sea floor to warn coasts.
fact: Tsunamis can also be caused by landslides, volcanic eruptions and, very rarely, meteorite impacts.
fact: About 8,000 years ago an underwater landslide off Norway, the Storegga Slide, sent a tsunami onto Scotland and the low land of Doggerland, now under the North Sea.
fact: Tsunamis are sometimes called tidal waves, but they have nothing to do with tides.
fact: If the sea suddenly drains away or a strong earthquake is felt at the coast, the safe thing is to move to high ground or far inland at once.
exercise: What usually causes a tsunami? | An undersea earthquake suddenly moving a lot of water | A strong wind blowing hard over the open sea for many days | The tide coming in faster than usual | A big ship sailing too fast close to the shore at night
exercise: How fast does a tsunami travel in the deep ocean? | About 700-800 km/h, like a jet plane | About 7-8 km/h, about as fast as a person walking | About 70 km/h, like a car on an ordinary road | It does not move until it reaches land
exercise: How high is a tsunami often in the deep ocean? | Less than a metre | About 100 metres, like a tall tower | About 1 kilometre, taller than any hill | As high as a skyscraper
exercise: What happens to a tsunami as it reaches the shore? | It slows down and piles up, sometimes over 10 metres high | It speeds up and gets lower and flatter as the water gets shallow | It sinks to the sea floor | It turns into a giant whirlpool that pulls boats down
exercise: What warning sign often comes just before a tsunami? | The sea drains far back from the beach | The sea turns bright red along the shore | The tide comes in very slowly and quietly | Fish jump onto the sand to sing
exercise: Is the first wave of a tsunami always the biggest? | No, it comes as a train of waves and the first is often not the biggest | Yes, the first wave is always the worst and the rest are just ripples | Yes, a tsunami is only ever one wave | No, the very last wave is always the biggest one of the whole train
exercise: What caused the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami? | A magnitude 9.1 earthquake off Sumatra | A huge volcano erupting in Hawaii | A meteorite landing in the sea near India | A hurricane over Thailand
exercise: About how many people died in the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami? | About 230,000 in 14 countries | About 2,300, all of them in one country | About 23 million people around the world | About 230, and only in Sumatra
exercise: What did the 2011 tsunami in Japan damage besides killing about 18,000 people? | The Fukushima nuclear plant | The Great Wall of China near Beijing | The Panama Canal and its locks | Mount Everest's southern slopes
exercise: How do warning centres detect tsunamis? | Earthquake sensors and pressure gauges on the sea floor | Watching the Moon each night for a red glow | Counting the number of seagulls | Measuring the colour of the sea from planes high above
exercise: Besides earthquakes, what else can cause a tsunami? | Landslides, volcanic eruptions and very rarely meteorites | Strong sunshine and long heat waves warming the sea | Heavy rain over the sea | Big spring tides at full moon pushing the water up high
exercise: What was the Storegga Slide? | An underwater landslide off Norway that sent a tsunami to Scotland | A huge glacier that slid all the way from the mountains into London | An avalanche in the Alps | A mudslide that buried a Roman town in Italy under metres of earth
exercise: Why is "tidal wave" a wrong name for a tsunami? | Tsunamis have nothing to do with tides | Tsunamis only ever happen when the tide is high | Tsunamis are caused by the pull of the Moon | Tsunamis are always much smaller than tides
exercise: You are on a beach and the sea suddenly drains far away. What should you do? | Run to high ground or far inland at once | Walk out to collect the stranded fish | Take photos from the water's edge | Wait on the beach for the tide to return
exercise: Where was Doggerland? | Low land now under the North Sea | A small country in the Pacific | An island off Japan | A desert in north Africa
test: In a film, a surfer rides a giant curling tsunami into the beach. What's wrong with it? | A tsunami is more like the whole sea rising and rushing inland than a surfing wave | Nothing, that is exactly what tsunamis look like, a giant curling wave you can ride | Tsunamis only happen in rivers | Tsunamis are always far too small to ride, just a ripple on the beach
test: A sailor in the deep Pacific says a tsunami passed under his ship and he felt nothing. Could that be true? | Yes, out there it can be under a metre high | No, it would have sunk the ship at once | No, tsunamis never cross the deep oceans at all | Yes, because tsunamis travel underground
test: "sea went out like miles, loads of fish flapping, let's go grab some!!" What should they do? | No! Run to high ground now, a tsunami may be coming | Yes, it's safe until the next high tide comes in tonight | Yes, but only for ten minutes | Yes, the sea won't come back for days, so take your time
test: After the first wave, people walk back down to the shore to look. Why is that dangerous? | More waves come minutes apart and the next may be bigger | The first wave is always followed by a big fire on the beach | The beach will be too hot to walk on | It is not dangerous, once the first wave is gone the danger is over
test: Your mate says: "Tsunamis are just big tides, the Moon makes them." Put him right. | They're usually made by undersea earthquakes, not tides | He's right, a full moon makes tsunamis | They're made by strong winds, not the Moon | They're made by whales splashing about
test: What would happen if a big earthquake shook the sea floor off Chile? | A tsunami could cross the Pacific and hit faraway coasts hours later | Only Chile could ever be hit, as tsunamis cannot travel far from home | The tsunami would stop after a few kilometres | Nothing, tsunamis only happen near Asia
test: Why is a pressure gauge on the sea floor useful? | It notices the extra water of a passing tsunami and sends a warning | It measures how salty the sea is | It stops the tsunami from moving any further | It tells sailors when it is high tide so they can get into harbour safely
test: "8000 yrs ago a tsunami hit Scotland?? from what" | An underwater landslide off Norway, the Storegga Slide | A volcano erupting in Edinburgh, Arthur's Seat | A meteorite landing in the Thames | A storm that lasted a whole year
test: Why did the 2004 tsunami kill people in so many countries? | It spread across the whole Indian Ocean in hours | Fourteen earthquakes happened at the same moment | Each country had its own small tsunami | It travelled overland across Asia
test: Why is it hard to spot a tsunami from a ship in the deep ocean? | It is low and very long, so it looks like nothing much | It travels deep under the sea floor, out of sight | It only comes out at night | It is hidden by thick fog every time it crosses the ocean
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a tsunami? | A series of waves generated by the sudden displacement of a great volume of water | A single wave produced by the gravitational action of the Moon upon the ocean | A wave generated exclusively by persistent winds over the ocean | A rise in sea level occurring each day at high tide
exam: Identify the conduct appropriate to a person upon a beach who observes the sudden withdrawal of the sea. | Immediate removal to high ground or to a location far inland | Advance onto the exposed sea floor in order to observe it | Continued presence upon the beach until the return of the tide | Retreat to the water's edge in order to photograph the event
exam: It is given that a tsunami traverses the deep ocean. Ascertain its characteristics in respect of speed and height. | A speed comparable to that of a jet aircraft and a height frequently below one metre | A speed comparable to that of a pedestrian and a height of one hundred metres | A speed of zero until its arrival at the shore | A speed comparable to that of a bicycle and a height of several kilometres above the sea
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the term "tidal wave". | It is a misnomer, since tsunamis bear no relation to the tides | It is the correct scientific term for a tsunami of any kind | It denotes a tsunami caused by the Moon | It denotes a tsunami occurring only at high tide
